WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.639 --> 00:00:03.909 align:start position:0%
 
the lure of freedom  travel and simplicity in a snug  self-sustaining van  has given birth to a worldwide social  media phenomenon  which in part has been fueled by the  pandemic the hashtag van life generates  millions of pictures and videos  of nomads in the retrofitted vans parked  on beaches  opening up to stunning views or  underneath starry skies  for some it's freedom and others it's  necessity  madison mcnair and her husband rainer  spent about two years living out of  their van  in 2020 after 120 000 kilometers  the couple decided to share their skills  with others they opened a van conversion  shop  in a small town on the southeastern  shore of georgian bay  in ontario  we had just started construction when  the pandemic was declared and i  honestly thought it was like the end we  made the wrong investment we're going to  be in trouble  [Music]  but kovitt has only accelerated business  canadians are fleeing lockdowns and big  mortgages and rent for 80 square feet  mobile living spaces  to roam and work wherever and whenever  they want  madison says they can barely keep up  with the demand  it's been really incredible so we're  already booking into 2022  and 2023 now it seems laughable that i  ever thought there would be a decline  because of course if you're stuck at  home you're suddenly looking at your  backyard realizing we  live in an incredible region why not go  road trip  for some it is the allure of the  ultimate trip my name is drew  my name is max i'm zara margie  we're a family from vancouver we just  moved into a  60 square foot living space we're gonna  see if he can survive  and not kill each other the nine family  from bc  hit the road in 2019 for a two year  adventure  they decided they wanted their children  to learn about the world  firsthand when we caught up with them  they were parked at a dive shop in crete  greece  how did you end up your entire family  becoming van lifers  we just want to travel we want to find a  way that's  most economical for us and spend more  time with the family  we were trying to get away from the  rat race and just live  life live life to the fullest we wanted  a van or a vehicle  that we the four of us can live  comfortably so we actually  ended up building one our van name is  yolo just because we only live once  and that's sort of our motto  touring ancient ruins allowed the kids  who were being homeschooled to see  history  come to life their days were packed with  adventure  let's go back to march 2020 when the  pandemic hit  and the world shuts down what was going  through your mind  we weren't sure if it was how the  pandemic was going to affect  canada the borders morocco where we were  at the time  we even talked about should we come back  and finish this trip but  we just contemplated and decided you  know what  everyone in the world is living the same  thing  we just need to adapt but adapting in  some ways seemed easier for the family  the simplicity of van lifestyle the  self-containment and self-sufficiency  allowed them to stay safe while  traveling during covet  this is a very useful storage we have  for quick access  things that we would need every square  inch fits our lifestyle and we learn how  to live minimally  so the kids learn to bring  only what brings joy to them give me an  example  of how the pandemic has affected your  life as van lifers because  of different lockdown scenarios and  people are concerned about traveling  all the tourist spots are practically  empty so we  have these beautiful places almost to  ourselves and enjoy it without  the crowds and the lineups we've been  pretty lucky about that van dwelling  van lifeing vehicle residents is not a  new concept  there are versions peppered throughout  history one example is this land yacht  created for travel in the early 1900s  another is the popular volkswagen  microbus synonymous with hippie  counterculture of the 1960s and 70s  i think it was kind of one of the funny  things for us getting into van life and  me on instagram hashtag van life at  first  you feel like this pioneer creating this  new way  and then we met a lovely woman in the  desert of arizona and she concluded  so you're the same as i was when i was  in my youth we just had a crappy vw that  didn't work very well ultimately this is  the version of getting mobility getting  flexibility  going after whatever the gold rush is  that you're going after  it's quite natural and it feels really  natural  when you're in it  squamish bc an adventurer's playground  of majestic mountains  and rivers for renowned rock climber  thomasina pidgeon  this is home i started living in a van  in kind of like when i was 20 when i  moved away from home  it just feels super natural it feels  like the way that i'm supposed to live  it just feels right to me like  when i'm in a house i feel really  restricted i don't feel that comfortable  when i'm in a vehicle i just feel more  free that's the affordable  campground for more than 25 years  thomasina has been a vehicle resident  and celebrating the freedom it allows  her this is also another place that  we stay at some time she owns a business  in town  and lives in this 1998 toyota sienna  with her 14 year old daughter  for me it's a choice people that rent  houses choose to pay rent so they choose  to have a mortgage because that has the  lifestyle that they want and the  conveniences they want  i don't even want those conveniences for  me that's like an economic freedom too  i think people just don't need that much  stuff peter's clothes and  our book while the 46 year old prefers  this form of living  others choose it out of necessity the  last year of job losses the inability to  pay rent  and the red-hot housing market has  forced more people into vehicle living  even if thomasina wanted to she couldn't  afford the skyrocketing rent in squamish  there's people that are doing it because  they just have no choice  and then there's people like me i mean i  actually i don't really make that much  money i own my own business but  the amount of money that i make is below  poverty level i think in march i paid  myself six bucks an hour it's like i  couldn't even  pay rent if i wanted to but i don't want  to you know some food  uh yeah just some teas some  jurisdictions have rules prohibiting it  so it's unclear just how many people  live in their vehicles across canada  and as the affordable housing crisis  worsens tensions between cities and van  dwellers seem to be growing  you see them throughout parts of metro  vancouver people living not  in homeless camps but in rvs after  letting the issue  slide for much of the pandemic the city  of vancouver is now cracking down  some municipalities are cracking down on  rvs trailers and vans illegally parked  on streets  squamish is no exception passing by-law  changes that would prevent people  sleeping in their vehicle overnight  in certain public spaces thomasina  co-directs the advocacy group  fighting the bylaw it penalizes people  for doing something which is a basic  human right which is to sleep  it's just a really exclusive bylaw that  to me is driven by stigma karen elliott  is the mayor of squamish she says the  amendments are to manage the tourist  population  and not aimed at long-term vehicle  residents it is not targeting  people that call squamish home full time  it really is about managing the influx  of thousands  of vehicles each summer and thousands of  people that are looking for a place to  stay  the mayor hopes the bylaws encourage  visitors to sleep at camp  sites this is also an option for vehicle  residents  we understand that that vehicle sleeping  might have to be a reality for some  folks but for this summer  we needed a solution for the visitor  management  and that for us is the priority as for  thomasina she plans to live in her van  indefinitely and won't change because of  the bylaw why should i have to hide why  do people always have to hide and  pretend that they don't exist because  people don't agree with a certain type  of lifestyle like to me that's just  wrong like i wish that people would just  be more open-minded and not be so  judgmental to a different way of living  that it's actually  pretty minimal and simple and to me it's  a beautiful lifestyle  back at madison and raynor's shop  there's no slowing  down and they know the van life trend  will continue  out of choice or necessity rent's  expensive  jobs are in stable how do i sign a  12-month lease  if my job won't give me a 12-month  stability how do i pick a place to live  these vehicles are far more about  freedom  self-control autonomy there's this  balancing act that i hope we find  there's ways we can adjust and see the  benefits for this  the employment opportunities are  important and giving  canadians the opportunity to find where  they belong  is important

